#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bale — Claude session orchestrator.

v0.1.0 — the first proper release. Cuts the v0.1 contract from
BALE.md §13: every piece of the pack and apply pipelines, plus the
ancillary commands (revert, retry, unlock, handoff, config init),
is now landed. v0.1.0 itself bumps VERSION, lands two small
quality-of-life fixes — `--include` and `--exclude` accept multiple
paths per flag (was: one flag per path) — and pairs with two
TARBALL.md tightenings (§5.2's example aligned to the literal-string
preflight; the §13.4 phase summary updated to reflect the
release-tarball packaging script having landed). The v0.0.x line
above remains the bootstrap narrative; the v0.1.0 milestone is
purely the cut.

Tracking incrementally toward the v0.1 contract: apply.sh handling
and post-apply manifest reconciliation (BALE.md §11 rule 18) landed
in v0.0.2; pack threshold caps (BALE.md §7.4) and the --force
override landed in v0.0.3; CODE.md
was added as a fourth global doc in v0.0.4, with the injection list,
install layout, and self-check scripts updated to match. The
configurables loader/merger and the `bale config init` wizard were
extracted from this file into the sibling `bale_config.py` module in
v0.0.4 to apply CODE.md §4.2 to the largest two sections; this file
now hosts the pack/apply/revert/retry/unlock/handoff pipelines, the
hook runtime, and the CLI surface. `bale unlock` (BALE.md §9.3) landed
in v0.0.5. `bale handoff` plus apply-time branching on
`response_kind: "bailout"` (TARBALL.md §5.6.3) landed in v0.0.6.
v0.0.7 finishes two follow-ups: `bale handoff` now pre-packs source
files extracted from handoff.md's `## Reading plan for the next
session` section (so the next request lands with the files Claude
recommended already in context/, instead of just handoff.md), and the
apply walkthrough's `[r]` revert action — previously a v0.1 stub that
told the user to run `bale revert <sid>` themselves — is wired
through to _discard_hold_state + clear_lock so the action completes
inline. v0.0.8 lands the BALE.md §10 git-init walkthrough: `bale pack`
in a non-git directory now offers to initialize the repo, prompts for
missing git identity (via bale_config.walkthrough_git_identity),
writes a .gitignore with the §6.4 baked-in exclusion set, and records
an initial commit before continuing into the rest of the pack
pipeline. v0.0.9 lands two coupled BALE.md §7.3 items: the
interactive pack wizard (`bale pack` with goal or --slug missing now
prompts on a TTY for the missing fields, plus constraints,
out_of_scope, and an optional README) and $EDITOR integration for the
README step. The editor invocation that `bale handoff --edit-goal`
introduced in v0.0.7 was generalized into a shared `open_in_editor`
helper so the pack wizard's README step and the handoff goal-edit
share one implementation. v0.0.10 lands the final BALE.md §7.3/§7.4
items: `.baleignore` reading (BALE.md §6.4) threaded through the
pack filter chain and the apply-time path-safety check (BALE.md §11
rule 14), the wizard's previously-deferred "anything to exclude"
prompt (now seeded with a preview of any persisted .baleignore so
the user sees what's already filtered before adding session-only
patterns), the §7.4 soft-cap `[e] edit excludes` branch (collect
more patterns, rebuild matcher, re-walk), a `--exclude` CLI flag
parallel to `--include`, and a `bale config init` step that walks
the user through `.baleignore` line-by-line (canonical persistence
surface, paired with the session-only wizard prompts above). With
the release-tarball packaging script (`scripts/build.sh`) landed
in bale-src and v0.1.0 bumped here, the v0.1 contract is closed.

v0.1.1 lands JSON Schema files for the three wire-format manifests
(request, response, diagnostics) under a new top-level `schemas/`
directory, and validates against them at pre-flight (BALE.md §11
row 6). The schemas are canonical for manifest *shape*; TARBALL.md
prose stays canonical for field semantics. bale's cross-field
invariants (sha256-by-action, claims ⊆ validation_will_run,
stripped-non-empty reasons, the bailout-empties rules, path-safety)
stay in Python, layered on top of the schema pass — so
validate_response_manifest now reads as "schema for shape, Python
for invariants", and the duplicated `_VALID_ACTIONS` /
`_VALID_CLAIM_VALUES` / key-set constants it used were pruned in
favor of the schema enums as single source of truth. A minimal
stdlib-only JSON Schema validator (no `jsonschema` dependency,
matching the stdlib-only constraint) backs the check; it supports
just the keyword subset the schemas use. `bale pack` and `bale
handoff` now schema-check the request manifests they build (defense
in depth, CODE.md §8.2), and the bailout apply path schema-checks
diagnostics.json where it previously only confirmed the file parsed.
The `schemas/` directory joins bin/ and docs/ in install.sh /
validate.sh / scripts/reinstall.sh layout checks and mirroring.

v0.1.2 extracts the manifest + JSON Schema validation cluster
(`load_schema`, the schema-validator worker and its helpers,
`validate_against_schema`, `validate_manifest_shape`, and the
request/response/diagnostics validators) out of section 16's
apply-helpers into a sibling `bin/bale_validate.py` module, the
extraction sibling of the v0.0.4 `bale_config` move, to bring section 16
back under CODE.md §4.2's size threshold. Behavior-preserving: the
validators keep their signatures, and `bin/bale`'s call sites are
unchanged — the three public entry points are pulled back into this
file's namespace via `from bale_validate import ...` so callers still
write `validate_response_manifest(manifest)` unqualified. The shared
`fail` helper is imported lazily from `__main__` inside each validator,
matching `bale_config`'s pattern; the `SCHEMAS_DIR` path constant and
the three schema-filename constants moved into the new module with the
cluster (they had no other consumer here). `bin/bale_validate.py` joins
`bin/bale` and `bin/bale_config.py` in the install.sh / validate.sh /
scripts/reinstall.sh layout checks. `check_response_shell_syntax` is an
apply-time `bash -n` pre-flight, not a schema/manifest validator, so it
stayed in section 16.

v0.1.3 extracts section 16's staging + reconciliation cluster
(`check_response_shell_syntax`, `verify_files_against_manifest`,
`stage_response`, `_walk_tree_sha256`, `reconcile_staging_against_manifest`,
`run_validation_sh`, `apply_changes_to_worktree`) out of the apply-helpers
section into a sibling `bin/bale_staging.py` module — the third extraction
sibling after the v0.0.4 `bale_config` and v0.1.2 `bale_validate` moves,
continuing to bring section 16 under CODE.md §4.2's threshold. Behavior-
preserving: the functions keep their signatures and `bin/bale`'s call sites
are unchanged — the six public entry points are pulled back into this file's
namespace via `from bale_staging import ...` (by name, matching
`bale_validate` so the apply-pipeline callers stay unqualified, the
convention chosen over `bale_config`'s qualified style because it leaves the
call sites untouched). `_walk_tree_sha256` is private to the cluster (only
`reconcile_staging_against_manifest` calls it) and is not re-exported. The
functions need no path constants from this file — every path derives from
their arguments — so unlike the `bale_validate` move nothing but the
functions themselves moved. `check_response_shell_syntax`, which v0.1.2 left
in section 16 because it isn't a manifest validator, moves here: an apply-
time pre-flight belongs with the staging cluster it gates. `bin/bale_staging.py`
joins `bin/bale`, `bin/bale_config.py`, and `bin/bale_validate.py` in the
install.sh / validate.sh / scripts/reinstall.sh layout checks. Section 16 now
holds `current_branch`, `working_tree_clean`, and the apply-walkthrough /
bailout / tarball-resolution helpers.

v0.2.0 cuts the BALE.md §13 v0.2 rollback phase: the `bale rollback [sid]`
command (`git revert` of an applied/merged session, BALE.md §9.2), with
`--undo` (revert the revert, re-applying), `--list` (applied sessions with
rollback status), `--stash` (auto-stash a dirty tree around the revert), and
merge-vs-plain-commit detection driving the `-m 1` mainline selection.
Conflicts leave the revert in progress with the affected files printed
(§9.2 step 5). The feature lands as a fourth sibling module,
`bin/bale_rollback.py` — net-new code rather than an extraction, so this file
gains only the CLI wiring (subparser + `from bale_rollback import
cmd_rollback`); see that module's docstring and the v0.2 response notes for
the layout rationale. Landing rollback also retires the three "v0.0.1 has no
`bale rollback` — use `git revert applied/<sid>` manually" stubs in
`cmd_revert` / `_discard_hold_state`, which now point at `bale rollback
<sid>`. `bin/bale_rollback.py` joins the other three bin/ modules in the
install.sh / validate.sh / scripts/reinstall.sh layout checks.

v0.2.1 starts the BALE.md §13 v0.3 "polish" phase, landing its four
apply-side inspection flags while deferring the phase's other two items
(inline `bale help <cmd>` and optional bash completion) to a v0.3b
follow-up — so v0.3 is not yet cut. The four flags all hang off the
`apply` subparser. `--show-validator` and `--show-apply-script` are pure
inspection: they resolve the tarball through `apply.search_paths` (like a
real apply), extract it, and print the requested script(s) to stdout, then
exit 0 — no session lock, no clean-tree requirement, no git side effects
(the new `inspect_response_scripts` helper in section 18). `--dry-run`
runs the read-only front half of the apply pipeline — extract, shell-syntax
pre-flight, manifest schema validation, `responds_to` match, and the
presence/sha256/path-safety verification — then prints a plan summary
(`format_dry_run_report`, section 18) and returns before the session
stamp, staging, branch creation, validation run, or commit; it requires a
lock (the `responds_to` check is part of "what would apply do") but not a
clean tree, since it touches neither the worktree nor `.bale/`. A dry-run
of a bailout response reports that apply would package a handoff rather
than running `_apply_bailout`. `--verbose` threads through
`_apply_pipeline` into `run_validation_sh` (bale_staging) and flips that
helper's output routing: default keeps `validation.sh` output in the
session log only (the walkthrough summary carries the verdict, BALE.md
§8.5 step 4), while `--verbose` additionally streams it live to the
terminal via a Popen line-reader. `_apply_pipeline` gained `dry_run` and
`verbose` keyword params, both defaulting False so `cmd_retry`'s call site
is unchanged. Wiring `--verbose` across other commands (BALE.md §5.4 lists
it as a global flag) and the §7.4 pass-through of `--verbose` into
`validation.sh` itself are left as future work — see this session's
notes.md.

v0.2.2 closes the two BALE.md §13 v0.3 polish items deferred in v0.2.1:
`bale help [<cmd>]` (inline discovery — BALE.md §5.1) and `bale
completion bash` (an opt-in bash completion generator — BALE.md §3.1).
Both are projections of one source of truth: the argparse parser
`build_parser()` returns. `cmd_help` dispatches argparse's own
`format_help()` after walking `_SubParsersAction.choices` to the named
command (one level for top-level commands, two for `config init` /
`help <cmd> <sub>`). `cmd_completion` walks the same parser once and
emits a self-contained bash function that does pure-shell dispatch at
Tab-press time — no subprocess back to bale per Tab press. The two
functions plus a small introspection layer (`_get_subparsers_action`,
`_iter_flag_actions`, `_classify_flag`, `_generate_bash_completion`)
land in a new section 24 "Help and completion"; the prior section 24
"CLI" renumbers to 25 and gains the two new subparsers. No separate
command/flag registry data structure was added: argparse already
encodes commands, flags, choices, and help strings, and a parallel
dict that `build_parser()` consumes would double the bookkeeping for
every new command and let drift creep in silently. With both items
landed, the v0.3 polish phase has one open thread — the `--verbose`
extension to commands beyond `apply` and the §7.4 pass-through to
`validation.sh` — so v0.3 is not cut here; the architect's call on
whether to cut now with that thread tracked separately, or to bundle
it into v0.3 first, is left to the next session.

v0.2.3 adds `bale status`, a read-only dashboard for the working
directory's bale (section 25, inserted ahead of the CLI parser, which
renumbers 25->26). It reports the tool version, the repo and working-tree
cleanliness, the open session and its lifecycle state (idle/packed/held/
orphan, BALE.md §9.5), the outbox, a light pointer at applied history
(deferring the full applied/reverted/re-applied view to `bale rollback
--list`), and config presence plus effective hooks and apply search paths.
It takes no lock and makes no git or filesystem writes, degrades gracefully
outside a git repo and on a malformed bale.toml, and always exits 0 on a
successful read -- problems surface as rows, not a failing exit code. Like
`help` and `completion` it carries no flags and so surfaces in both for
free (all three read the same `build_parser()`). Structure follows the
file's build-a-string / caller-prints split: a pure `_session_state_and_hint`
classifier and a pure `_render_status`, with `_gather_status` doing the only
I/O -- the seam ADR 0003's two test tiers anticipate, though the tests
themselves stay deferred to the v0.4 harness (ADR 0001). `bale status` is
net-new surface not in BALE.md's §13 build map; whether it folds into the
still-open v0.3 cut or stands alone is the architect's call (see this
session's notes.md).

Per BALE.md section 13, the v0.0.x line implements just enough to
apply its own response tarballs, plus the configurables wizard that
successive bale-on-bale sessions evolve:

    bale pack <goal> --include <paths>
    bale apply <tarball>
    bale revert <sid>
    bale rollback [sid]
    bale retry <tarball>
    bale unlock
    bale handoff <bailout-tarball>
    bale config init

Rollback (`bale rollback`) landed in v0.2 — `git revert` of an applied
session, with `--undo` / `--list` / `--stash`. It lives in the sibling
`bale_rollback` module; this file holds only its CLI wiring. Stdlib only —
which since v0.1.0's 3.10-compat work means Python 3.10+ (3.11+ uses stdlib
`tomllib`; 3.10 uses the vendored parser in `bin/_bale_toml.py`, both behind
`bale_config`).

Sections:
   1. Imports + constants                              (~line  339)
   2. Logging                                          (~line  416)
   3. Shell / git helpers                              (~line  503)
   4. Hashing                                          (~line  542)
   5. Session IDs and the per-day counter              (~line  558)
   6. Lock handling                                    (~line  616)
   7. .gitignore handling for the .bale/ directory     (~line  647)
   8. Path-safety checks                               (~line  677)
   9. Editor invocation                                (~line  725)
  10. Pack: file enumeration and filtering             (~line  786)
  11. Pack: scope projection + threshold caps (BALE.md section 7.4) (~line 1163)
  12. Pack: slug validation                            (~line 1446)
